1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,400
Ice melts on a warm afternoon.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,683
The same water freezes in a freezer.

3
00:00:04,683 --> 00:00:06,477
One change, two directions.

4
00:00:06,877 --> 00:00:08,617
Only the temperature changed.

5
00:00:08,617 --> 00:00:10,599
So temperature must have a vote.

6
00:00:11,049 --> 00:00:14,531
Aarav drops ice into his water
bottle in the college corridor.

7
00:00:14,531 --> 00:00:15,954
In minutes, it melts.

8
00:00:16,304 --> 00:00:19,326
Last time, we had to count
the surroundings too.

9
00:00:19,326 --> 00:00:20,318
That is hard.

10
00:00:20,818 --> 00:00:24,717
By the end, you will know one number
about the reaction alone that predicts

11
00:00:24,717 --> 00:00:26,699
its direction, and where it stops.

12
00:00:27,349 --> 00:00:29,533
Think of a tug of war with two teams.

13
00:00:29,883 --> 00:00:31,411
Team one is enthalpy.

14
00:00:31,411 --> 00:00:34,011
Giving out heat pulls a change forward.

15
00:00:34,411 --> 00:00:35,965
Team two is entropy.

16
00:00:35,965 --> 00:00:38,427
More disorder pulls it forward too.

17
00:00:38,827 --> 00:00:41,153
Temperature is a booster for team two.

18
00:00:41,153 --> 00:00:43,942
The hotter it gets, the
harder entropy pulls.

19
00:00:44,542 --> 00:00:48,673
Let's put ice on our molecule bench
and turn the temperature knob.

20
00:00:49,023 --> 00:00:50,530
Melting takes heat in.

21
00:00:50,530 --> 00:00:52,426
Plus six kilojoules per mole.

22
00:00:52,426 --> 00:00:54,076
Team enthalpy pulls back.

23
00:00:54,476 --> 00:00:56,832
But melting makes the water messier.

24
00:00:56,832 --> 00:00:59,647
Plus twenty two joules
per kelvin per mole.

25
00:00:59,647 --> 00:01:01,445
Team entropy pulls forward.

26
00:01:01,845 --> 00:01:04,297
At minus ten degrees, enthalpy wins.

27
00:01:04,297 --> 00:01:05,401
The ice stays.

28
00:01:05,801 --> 00:01:07,103
Turn the knob up.

29
00:01:07,103 --> 00:01:11,686
At zero degrees, two seventy three
kelvin, the teams are exactly equal.

30
00:01:11,686 --> 00:01:13,532
Ice and water sit together.

31
00:01:13,932 --> 00:01:16,256
At twenty five degrees, entropy wins.

32
00:01:16,256 --> 00:01:17,282
The ice melts.

33
00:01:17,882 --> 00:01:21,320
The score of this tug of war is the
Gibbs energy, named after Josiah Willard

34
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:22,220
Gibbs.

35
00:01:22,102 --> 00:01:25,342
Delta G equals delta H minus T delta S.

36
00:01:25,742 --> 00:01:29,422
If delta G is negative,
the change is spontaneous.

37
00:01:29,422 --> 00:01:31,375
If positive, it is not.

38
00:01:31,375 --> 00:01:34,273
If zero, the system is at equilibrium.

39
00:01:34,923 --> 00:01:36,377
Why does this work?

40
00:01:36,377 --> 00:01:37,467
Step by step.

41
00:01:37,817 --> 00:01:38,717
Step one.

42
00:01:38,418 --> 00:01:41,610
The total entropy is the system's
change plus the surroundings' change.

43
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:43,260
Step two.

44
00:01:43,133 --> 00:01:46,331
The surroundings gain the
heat the system gives out.

45
00:01:46,331 --> 00:01:48,764
Their change is minus delta H over T.

46
00:01:49,514 --> 00:01:50,587
Step three.

47
00:01:50,587 --> 00:01:53,034
Multiply everything by minus T.

48
00:01:53,034 --> 00:01:57,252
Minus T delta S total equals
delta H minus T delta S.

49
00:01:57,252 --> 00:01:58,756
That is delta G.

50
00:01:59,506 --> 00:02:02,859
So total entropy going up
means delta G going down.

51
00:02:02,859 --> 00:02:05,089
One rule, seen from the system.

52
00:02:05,839 --> 00:02:07,288
Now the four cases.

53
00:02:07,638 --> 00:02:09,605
Heat out and more disorder.

54
00:02:09,605 --> 00:02:11,354
Both teams pull forward.

55
00:02:11,354 --> 00:02:13,570
Spontaneous at every temperature.

56
00:02:13,970 --> 00:02:15,854
Heat in and less disorder.

57
00:02:15,854 --> 00:02:17,056
Both pull back.

58
00:02:17,056 --> 00:02:18,350
Never spontaneous.

59
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:20,675
Heat out but less disorder.

60
00:02:20,675 --> 00:02:23,522
Spontaneous only when cold, like freezing.

61
00:02:23,922 --> 00:02:25,772
Heat in but more disorder.

62
00:02:25,772 --> 00:02:28,482
Spontaneous only when hot, like melting.

63
00:02:29,082 --> 00:02:29,982
Compare.

64
00:02:29,775 --> 00:02:32,515
Freezing water: heat out, less disorder.

65
00:02:32,515 --> 00:02:35,060
Melting ice: heat in, more disorder.

66
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:36,644
Pause and think.

67
00:02:36,644 --> 00:02:39,012
Melting ice at fifty degrees Celsius.

68
00:02:39,012 --> 00:02:41,139
Is delta G positive or negative?

69
00:02:44,339 --> 00:02:45,239
Negative.

70
00:02:45,046 --> 00:02:49,540
Fifty degrees is above two seventy
three kelvin, so the entropy team wins.

71
00:02:50,190 --> 00:02:51,500
Now, equilibrium.

72
00:02:51,850 --> 00:02:56,486
The standard Gibbs energy change tells
us how far a reaction goes, through

73
00:02:56,486 --> 00:02:58,329
the equilibrium constant K.

74
00:02:58,729 --> 00:03:03,166
Delta G naught equals minus
RT times the natural log of K.

75
00:03:03,166 --> 00:03:07,670
Or minus two point three oh
three RT log K, in base ten.

76
00:03:08,070 --> 00:03:12,670
R is the gas constant, eight point
three one four joules per kelvin per

77
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:13,570
mole.

78
00:03:13,637 --> 00:03:16,457
A big negative delta G
naught means a huge K.

79
00:03:16,457 --> 00:03:17,521
Mostly products.

80
00:03:17,521 --> 00:03:19,731
A big positive one means a tiny K.

81
00:03:19,731 --> 00:03:20,840
Mostly reactants.

82
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:22,563
Remember this.

83
00:03:22,563 --> 00:03:24,754
Negative delta G, spontaneous.

84
00:03:24,754 --> 00:03:26,191
Zero, equilibrium.

85
00:03:26,891 --> 00:03:28,581
Now, one worked example.

86
00:03:28,881 --> 00:03:33,711
Oxygen turning into ozone at two
ninety eight kelvin has K equal to two

87
00:03:33,711 --> 00:03:37,464
point four seven times ten
to the minus twenty nine.

88
00:03:37,464 --> 00:03:39,085
Find delta G naught.

89
00:03:39,585 --> 00:03:43,160
Log of K is about minus twenty
eight point six oh seven.

90
00:03:43,910 --> 00:03:48,609
Two point three oh three, times eight
point three one four, times two ninety

91
00:03:48,609 --> 00:03:49,509
eight.

92
00:03:49,195 --> 00:03:51,819
About five seven oh six joules per mole.

93
00:03:52,569 --> 00:03:56,630
Multiply by minus twenty eight point
six oh seven, with the minus sign

94
00:03:56,630 --> 00:03:57,530
in front.

95
00:03:57,381 --> 00:03:59,875
Plus one sixty three kilojoules per mole.

96
00:04:00,625 --> 00:04:03,009
Hugely positive, so K is tiny.

97
00:04:03,009 --> 00:04:06,062
Oxygen hardly turns into ozone on its own.

98
00:04:06,712 --> 00:04:08,173
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:04:08,523 --> 00:04:12,103
Students put delta H in kilojoules
and delta S in joules straight into

100
00:04:12,103 --> 00:04:13,003
the formula.

101
00:04:13,305 --> 00:04:14,269
Convert first.

102
00:04:14,269 --> 00:04:16,530
Six kilojoules is six thousand joules.

103
00:04:16,530 --> 00:04:17,493
Then subtract.

104
00:04:18,143 --> 00:04:19,686
Where is this asked?

105
00:04:19,686 --> 00:04:22,678
Boards ask what the sign of delta G means.

106
00:04:22,678 --> 00:04:27,078
Entrance tests ask the temperature
where a reaction turns spontaneous,

107
00:04:27,078 --> 00:04:28,259
or ask for K.

108
00:04:28,859 --> 00:04:30,062
Exam level now.

109
00:04:30,062 --> 00:04:34,796
A reaction has delta H plus one seventy
eight kilojoules per mole and delta

110
00:04:34,796 --> 00:04:37,846
S plus one sixty joules
per kelvin per mole.

111
00:04:37,846 --> 00:04:40,560
Above what temperature is it spontaneous?

112
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:41,815
Pause and solve.

113
00:04:47,215 --> 00:04:48,562
The answer is B.

114
00:04:48,562 --> 00:04:51,069
About eleven hundred thirteen kelvin.

115
00:04:51,569 --> 00:04:55,356
It becomes spontaneous when
delta G drops below zero.

116
00:04:55,356 --> 00:04:57,865
Set delta H equal to T delta S.

117
00:04:58,365 --> 00:04:59,265
Convert.

118
00:04:58,923 --> 00:05:02,427
One seventy eight kilojoules is one
lakh seventy eight thousand joules.

119
00:05:02,927 --> 00:05:06,954
T equals one seventy eight
thousand divided by one sixty.

120
00:05:06,954 --> 00:05:09,293
Eleven twelve point five kelvin.

121
00:05:09,293 --> 00:05:10,915
A forgot to convert.

122
00:05:11,515 --> 00:05:13,055
Here is your recap.

123
00:05:13,055 --> 00:05:14,637
Screenshot this card.

124
00:05:17,837 --> 00:05:20,024
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

125
00:05:20,424 --> 00:05:21,324
One.

126
00:05:20,876 --> 00:05:23,207
What does a negative delta G tell you?

127
00:05:24,007 --> 00:05:24,907
Two.

128
00:05:24,408 --> 00:05:27,070
Why does melting ice need
a high enough temperature?

129
00:05:27,870 --> 00:05:28,770
Three.

130
00:05:28,466 --> 00:05:32,449
If delta G naught is large and
negative, is K big or small?

131
00:05:33,349 --> 00:05:36,128
Next time: a reaction reaches equilibrium.

132
00:05:36,128 --> 00:05:39,332
Does it really stop, or
keep running both ways?

133
00:05:39,332 --> 00:05:42,072
We find out in Chapter six, Equilibrium.
